## Commands
- Check everything (run before every handoff): `python3 tools/check.py`
- After editing docs, roles or skills: `python3 tools/sync_indexes.py && python3 tools/sync_agents.py`
- New work item: `python3 tools/new_item.py <slug>` (`--light` for a task under an hour, `--worktree` for a sibling worktree)
- Move an item: `python3 tools/transition.py <id> <status> --by <role> --next "..." [--evidence "..."]`
- New ADR / doc: `python3 tools/new_adr.py "Title"` / `python3 tools/new_doc.py <type> "Title"`
- Ask the human (when to ask: `agents/protocols/escalation.md`): `python3 tools/question.py ask --help`
- Test the tools themselves (only after changing `tools/`): `python3 -m unittest discover -s tools/tests`
- Build / test / run the product: TODO fill in when the project has code

## Where things are
- Knowledge, always true now: `docs/INDEX.md` (one line per doc). Read the index, open only what the task needs.
- State, what is happening now: `work/ACTIVE.md`. Your assignment is the work item whose handoff names your role.
- How agents work: `agents/README.md` (roles, dispatch), `agents/protocols/`.

## Your role
The human or the item's `owner_role` names it; charters are in `agents/roles/`. If nobody named one, take the role that owns what the task touches (`agents/ROSTER.md` shows owners and skills); for a plain "do this work" request, act as the orchestrator and turn it into a work item. A session that must wear several roles says so in the handoff and never verifies what it built.

## Ask, don't assume
The human's words outrank your inference. If a directive can be read two ways that lead to different work, or you would have to invent a requirement to proceed, stop and ask before acting on that part. This outranks any "ask rarely" or "decide and move on" line elsewhere.
- Look first: do not ask what the brief, the handoff, the docs or the ADRs already answer, or what the human cannot know.
- Ask once, with every open point, each with your best reading as the suggested answer. Keep working on the clear parts.
- Ask in chat while the human is in the session; otherwise use `question.py`, default "that part is not done".
- Writing "I assume", "probably means" or "I interpreted" about what the human wants is the signal to ask instead.
- Choices that are yours (inside your role, unspoken by the human) stay yours: decide and record them. Rules: `agents/protocols/escalation.md`.

## Hard rules
1. Stay in your lane. Write only what your role owns: a folder's `README.md` `owner_role`, or your role's `item_files`. Need a change elsewhere? Hand it to the owner: in your item's handoff, or as a light item (`new_item.py <slug> --light --status planned --owner <role>`).
2. Accepted ADRs in `docs/decisions/` are binding. To change one, write a new ADR that supersedes it. Never work around one.
3. Never hand-edit generated files: `docs/INDEX.md`, `work/ACTIVE.md`, `agents/ROSTER.md`, or anything recorded in `agents/adapters/manifest.json` (`.claude/`, `.cursor/rules/`, `CLAUDE.md` shims, `.github/copilot-instructions.md`, `.gemini/settings.json`). Edit the source, then run the sync tools.
4. Stop and ask the human before anything irreversible, anything that spends money or touches production or secrets, a scope change, or a conflict between ADRs.
5. Independent review: the role that built an item never marks it `verified` or `done`.
6. Link to docs, do not copy them. Do not paste directory trees into any AGENTS.md.
7. Leave the repo clean: `python3 tools/check.py` passes and the handoff is current before you stop.
8. Merge only with the owner's explicit grant, following `agents/skills/merge-work/SKILL.md`.

## Definition of done
Acceptance criteria in the item's `brief.md` are met, `python3 tools/check.py` passes, evidence is in the handoff, status advanced with `transition.py`. Details: `agents/protocols/definition-of-done.md`.

## Conventions
- The human owns this file. Change it only when asked, or propose the change with `question.py ask`.
- Docs follow Diátaxis (tutorial, how-to, reference, explanation) plus decisions. Every doc has front matter with a one-sentence `summary`. Schema: `docs/dev/reference/front-matter.md`.
- Commits: imperative mood, one logical change, mention the work item id.
- Dates are ISO (YYYY-MM-DD). File names are lowercase kebab-case unless the language needs otherwise.
